Done. `scorer_out.npz` and `NOTES.md` are written in the step 2 folder and verified with forward passes.

**→ Result.** Evidence goes from **15/32 to 29/32** correct. On held-out messages I wrote: **14/20** new PIN messages route to cards, and **2 of 276** non-PIN controls change (two messages per Banking77 intent plus near-confusers). The exchange-rate messages from step 1 keep their routing. Command: `final.py` in the scratchpad.

**→ What changed.** Only four hidden units (234, 534, 740, 972), all of which contributed under 0.001 to any logit before. They now hold two clipped threshold detectors: **PIN content on the account_security vector → that logit −8**, and **PIN content on the cards vector → +2** to break the cards-vs-disputes tie. Shapes, keys, dtypes, step-1 units and everything else are identical to the input.

**→ Why it took work.** Marker vectors are 99% two shared axes (match strength and mean direction). Plain mean differences separate nothing in any space, and minimal pairs do not help. What works is a **whitened** difference of means (PCA of my 110 generic messages, top 100 components to unit variance) within one option slice, plus an option-identity direction as a gate. The PIN signal is readable on the account_security vector, weak on the cards vector.

**→ Mistake: Guard.** My first implementation used the raw-vector extra module with a linear scale estimate. It misfired on strongly matching vectors. Guard: a faithfulness check (implemented logit delta vs intended step, max error now 0.02) runs before any file is accepted. Lesson is in NOTES.md and in a memory note. I did not edit the lab's own LESSONS.md, since that is your harness log and outside the task folder.

**→ Risk.** Collateral is estimated on 276 messages, so the true rate on 3,080 could be 1 to 3%. Recall on short fragments like "pin change" is the weak spot. I did not post to the work board, as this was a single-turn harness task.
